---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import OcrPdf from '../islands/OcrPdf';

const faqs = [
  { q: "What does OCR do?", a: "OCR (optical character recognition) reads the letters in a scan or photo and turns them into real text. A scanned PDF becomes searchable, and you can copy text from it." },
  { q: "Is my document uploaded to a server?", a: "No. Recognition runs in your browser with tesseract.js. The engine and language files are downloaded once from PDFSherpa itself, and your document never leaves your device." },
  { q: "Which languages are supported?", a: "English, Urdu and Hindi. For every language you get a searchable PDF (the scan with an invisible text layer) plus the text to copy or save as .txt. For Urdu and Hindi, how well copying from the PDF works depends on your PDF reader, so the .txt is the most reliable way to get the exact text." },
  { q: "How long does it take?", a: "About 2–10 seconds per page on a modern phone or computer, depending on the page and the device. The first run also downloads the engine (a few MB)." },
  { q: "How accurate is it?", a: "Clean printed scans are usually recognised very accurately. Handwriting, low-resolution scans and photos taken at an angle produce more mistakes, so check important text." },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="ocr-pdf"
  title="OCR PDF – Make Scanned PDFs Searchable Free | PDFSherpa"
  description="Turn scanned PDFs and photos into searchable PDFs and editable text. English, Urdu and Hindi OCR. Free and private: runs on your device."
  h1="OCR PDF"
  lead="Turn scanned PDFs and photos into text you can search, select and copy. Download a searchable PDF or plain text. Your files never leave your device."
  howToTitle="How to OCR a Scanned PDF"
  steps={["Select or drop a scanned PDF, or photos of pages.", "Choose the language: English, Urdu or Hindi.", "Click <strong>Recognise text</strong>, then download a searchable PDF, a .txt or copy the text."]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={["pdf-to-text", "scan-to-pdf", "compress-pdf", "pdf-to-markdown"]}
>
  <OcrPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
<h2>What OCR PDF does</h2>
    <p>
      A scanned PDF is just pictures of pages. You can’t search it, copy from it, or have it read aloud. OCR PDF recognises the text in every page and
      adds an invisible text layer on top of the original image. The page looks exactly the same, but now Ctrl+F finds words and you can select and copy text.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Add your file.</strong> A scanned PDF, or one or more photos of pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick the language</strong> the text is written in.</li>
      <li><strong>Recognise.</strong> Progress is shown page by page. The first run downloads the recognition engine once.</li>
      <li><strong>Download</strong> the searchable PDF or a .txt file, or copy the text. You can also correct the text before copying it.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>When you need OCR</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Searching long scanned contracts, books or reports for a word.</li>
      <li>Copying text from a scanned letter instead of retyping it.</li>
      <li>Making scans accessible to screen readers.</li>
      <li>Getting text from photos of notices, receipts and documents in Urdu or Hindi.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tips for better results</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Scan at 300 DPI if you can. With a phone, use <a href="/scan-to-pdf">Scan to PDF</a> and the Document look for sharp, high-contrast pages.</li>
      <li>Straighten sideways pages first with <a href="/rotate-pdf">Rotate PDF</a>.</li>
      <li>Crop dark borders with <a href="/crop-pdf">Crop PDF</a>. They can confuse recognition.</li>
      <li>If your PDF already has selectable text, you don’t need OCR. Use <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to Text</a> instead, which is instant.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Handwriting is recognised poorly. Complex layouts with columns and tables may come out in a different reading order. The searchable PDF stores
      pages as JPG images, so its size can differ from the original. Use <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a> afterwards if needed.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Most online OCR services upload your document to their servers. PDFSherpa runs the open-source Tesseract engine inside your browser, so even
      confidential scans stay on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
